{
  "analysis": "The review describes a board and inventory-capacity problem: too many new objects occupy limited space, leaving the game gridlocked, and archived objects still consume inventory slots. That is a concrete storage shortfall and fits Waste Aversion. The mention of insufficient energy does not state a regeneration timer or other required wait, so it does not establish Playing by Appointment. Gems are mentioned, but no real-money conversion is described. The ad mismatch is external marketing and is out of scope under R13. No other label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "game is now gridlocked. Way too many new things on a tiny board.",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory; boundary rule requires a concrete capacity shortfall or blockage.",
      "rationale": "The limited board cannot accommodate the growing number of items, producing an explicit storage shortfall that gridlocks play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an in-game advertisement appearing after every over, making ad viewing a recurring and forced part of play. The accidental tap, ad opening, app closure, and required restart are attributed to bad app behavior or a UI issue, so those technical faults do not independently receive a dark-pattern label under R11. The review does not describe a purchase prompt, deceptive control placement, or lost progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; compelled to view advertisements",
      "rationale": "An advertisement is imposed during gameplay after each over, making ad exposure a recurring forced interruption."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes intrusive in-game pop-up screens. The weekly challenge score screen is only mentioned as a pop-up and does not by itself establish a coded mechanic. The quit-confirmation screens must be dismissed with an X before the player can continue, which is an interface obstruction requiring the player to clear an unwanted screen. The increased cost is not tied to a specific purchase or progression mechanic, so it does not support a monetary label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "are you sure you want to quit screens that you have to X out of before continuing",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs UI complaint: assign when the interface obstructs an opt-out or requires a screen to be dismissed before continuing.",
      "rationale": "The game forces the player to dismiss quit-confirmation screens before resuming play, obstructing the exit-related interface flow."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes in-game advertisements and pop-ups appearing continuously in sequence before gameplay can begin. This makes the player sit through repeated ad exposure as a condition of reaching play, which is a forced-ad mechanic. The review does not identify purchase prompts, insufficient-funds prompts, or a specific interface trick, so no monetary UI label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the seemingly endless in-game ads and pop-ups that cascade one after another so much that by the time I can actually play",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is effectively compelled to encounter repeated in-game ads before being able to play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements appearing inside the game despite the advertised claim that it was ad free. More specifically, the player cannot dismiss an ad normally: exiting it requires opening the Google Play Store, which obstructs the opt-out and causes additional data use. The ad is therefore a compelled in-game advertisement, and the forced store redirect is an interface-level obstruction to dismissing it.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "definition; compelled to view advertisements",
      "rationale": "The player is required to interact with the advertisement and open the Play Store in order to exit it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule: attention-directing without an opt-out; an exit or dismissal is obstructed or routed elsewhere",
      "rationale": "The ad's dismissal path is obstructed by routing the player into the Play Store instead of providing a normal exit."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily streak mechanic that rewards continued daily play and resets the player's progress when the game marks the streak as broken. The player says the reset was erroneous, but under the bug boundary the underlying streak-reset penalty remains codable because it is a deliberate rule for missing a day. No separate progression time gate is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "said I broke my streak and though I did not, it sent me right back to the beginning",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and R11: daily streaks with a reset penalty remain codable even when a malfunction falsely triggers the reset",
      "rationale": "The game tracks a daily streak and resets it to the beginning when it considers a day missed, even though the reviewer says the detection was incorrect."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Event and Duel modes becoming unresponsive because of lag or a suspected network fault, followed by the game declaring a loss and deducting ranked points. The malfunction itself does not support a dark-pattern label under R11. Although the game applies a loss and point penalty, the review attributes that outcome to the technical failure and does not show that the same penalty would follow a deliberate exit or normal game rule. Duel Mode and ranked progress do indicate a competitive game structure, so Competition is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Duel Mode game",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Duel Mode describes gameplay structured around competition between players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three separate things. Loading takes too long, but this is a technical or service delay rather than a coded game-design mechanic, and there is no evidence of a fragmented download or a deliberate wait timer. The bonus feature is described as appearing rarely and failing to match, which implies an unpredictable or low-frequency reward outcome. The reviewer also explicitly says they are buying coins, which describes real money being converted into in-game currency. The term \"ww4\" is unclear, but identifying it is not necessary to support the reward-delivery label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the bonus lady rarely comes out or even matches with anything on ww4.",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards: unpredictable or random reward delivery; assign where the review describes the frequency or delivery of a bonus outcome.",
      "rationale": "The bonus feature appears infrequently and does not reliably produce a matching result, implying a variable reward mechanism."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins from them.",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs purchases denominated in currency: \"buy coins\" identifies the currency as the object acquired through a real-money purchase.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes purchasing in-game coins, establishing conversion from real money into virtual currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Cashman Casino Slots Games \"WW4\" \"bonus lady\"",
  "search_result": "The search did not reliably identify what \"WW4\" refers to in Cashman Casino, so no label depended on interpreting that term."
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several complaints, but most are evaluations or resource shortages rather than coded mechanics. The statement that other players cheat describes player conduct, not a game design that rewards harmful behavior. The complaints about Peg-E and the race car event only evaluate named events without clearly describing a qualifying mechanic. “Way to many gold duplicates!” refers to duplicate gold stickers delivered through MONOPOLY GO!'s randomized sticker-pack system, which matches Reward Mania's unwanted duplicate rewards. “Not enough trades a day!” explicitly describes a player-to-player trading limit, matching Reciprocity's resource-exchange indicator. Not enough dice and the 500-space review limit do not independently satisfy any listed label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Way to many gold duplicates!",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards; Reward Mania applies to unpredictable or randomized reward delivery and duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomized pool.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer reports receiving excessive duplicate gold stickers, an unwanted outcome of the game's randomized sticker reward system."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Not enough trades a day!",
      "rule_applied": "Definition and boundary rule vs Forced Fellowship: exchanging resources or items with people already playing is Reciprocity.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes a player-to-player trading mechanic and a limit on those resource exchanges."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "MONOPOLY GO gold stickers duplicate sticker packs",
  "search_result": "The search established that MONOPOLY GO has duplicate gold stickers and randomized sticker-pack outcomes, supporting the interpretation of “gold duplicates” as unwanted duplicate rewards from a random pool. ([play.google.com](https://play.google.com/store/apps/editorial?hl=en-US&id=mc_games_editorialevergreen_monopoly_go_all_about_stickers_postinstall_fcp&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a competitive board game with opponents whose rolls or counts are perceived as unequal: opponents receive good counts while the reviewer receives small counts. They also explicitly allege that the game determines losses regardless of player skill, describing a hidden or manipulated outcome rule rather than merely an unlucky streak. The mention of glitches is attributed to technical faults and does not itself support a dark-pattern label. The comments about time wasting, installing the game, and players reaching higher levels do not describe grinding, infinite progression, or a time-gated mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against opponents in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you are meant to lose, no matter how good you play you will definitley lose",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that outcomes are fixed or manipulated so player skill cannot affect the result."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game progression system where the player must repeatedly play a Candy Crush-style level to earn stars, including to advance Austin's story. Receiving only one star per game means progress depends on repeated play of the same type of activity, which supports Grinding. The loss of points after failing a level is a failure penalty, not a stopping or exit penalty, so it does not support Mandatory Marathon or Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. The misleading escape-game advertisement is third-party marketing and is outside the application's scope under R13, so it does not support Advertisement or another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Always you play one game you just get one star only. And all your points get lost if you don't make that level. Even for walking Austin you will have to earn a star by playing stupid game.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Slow Progress; Grinding applies where the player must repeat a named activity to make progress.",
      "rationale": "The player must repeatedly complete the same game to earn stars and advance Austin's progression, making the activity a required and tedious progress loop."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes advertisements encountered inside the game that cannot be dismissed. The missing or ineffective close control prevents continuation until the app is closed and restarted, so the ads are forced and the interface obstructs the opt-out path. No payment, progress loss, or deliberate session-forfeiture rule is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Can't close the ads, therefore can't continue playing",
      "rule_applied": "definition; forced to watch ads",
      "rationale": "The player cannot dismiss the in-game advertisements and therefore cannot continue playing normally."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Can't close the ads",
      "rule_applied": "boundary rule vs UI complaint; obstructed or absent opt-out",
      "rationale": "The interface provides no usable close control, obstructing the player's ability to opt out of the ad."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes purchasing a large pack of in-game coins and receiving a refund after the purchase was accidental. Buying a coin pack indicates real money being converted into virtual currency, supporting Intermediate Currency. The accidental nature alone does not establish an interface design such as missing confirmation, a default purchase, or a deceptive control, so Easy to Purchase is not assigned. The review does not explicitly describe wagering money or chance-based rewards, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "accidentally bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Boundary rules vs purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes acquiring in-game coins through a purchase, implying real money was exchanged for virtual currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game ad mechanic that offers rewards for watching advertisements. The failure to award those rewards is attributed to a connection problem, so that malfunction itself is not coded as a deliberate dark pattern under R11. The general mention of microtransactions does not identify a specific monetization mechanic beyond dissatisfaction, so it does not support another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "get rewards for the ads you watched",
      "rule_applied": "T_Advertisement indicator: players are incentivized to view advertisements through the promise of rewards; R11 does not suppress the underlying functional ad-reward mechanic when the failure to deliver it is attributed to a technical fault.",
      "rationale": "The game offers rewards in exchange for watching ads, even though a connection error prevents the rewards from being credited."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an idle base-builder with heroes, scouting other bases, and gathering resources. It explicitly mentions waiting for building times, which indicates in-game construction timers that make the player wait before continuing. The request for more mini games or periodic rewards describes desired additions, not currently present mechanics, so it is not coded. No competition, grinding, or reward mechanic is clearly described as an active design behavior.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "waiting for building times",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "Building timers impose a real-time wait before construction or related progress can continue."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is praising the graphics but describes a storage-size mismatch: the game is presented as having a 1GB size, while downloading or running it appears to consume enough space to fill the phone and is associated with roughly 30GB of game data. This is a technical storage requirement rather than a gameplay or monetization mechanic, so the applicable label is Fragmented Downloads.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "why you give the screen size 1GB my phone is storage full for this game I am playing this game on Red magic 11 Pro because I will download it meaning in 30 gb games on it",
      "rule_applied": "Small advertised App Store size vs. large mandatory in-game download",
      "rationale": "The span contrasts a stated 1GB game size with storage being filled and roughly 30GB of game data, implying that the true download requirement is much larger than advertised."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a resource-gated level system. Each level consumes credits, and when the player cannot afford the next level, the game imposes a one-hour wait before play can resume. The game also offers a paid alternative: spending real money to obtain credits and continue playing. The phrase about earning 2.8k credits three levels earlier reinforces that credits are an in-game currency acquired through gameplay, but it does not show stranded leftover value or a specific inventory shortfall, so Waste Aversion is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "leaves you waiting an hour to play again",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "When the player lacks enough credits to play, the game imposes a one-hour timer before another level can be played."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Skip / Pay to Progress: allowing or encouraging players to spend money to bypass an obstacle and continue progress.",
      "rationale": "Spending cash on credits is presented as the alternative to waiting, allowing the player to resume level progression immediately."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency indicator: real-world money is exchanged for in-game currency used for gameplay purchases.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes acquiring the in-game currency called credits with real money."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a 7-day streak that can be lost, which is a daily-return reward mechanic and therefore fits Daily Rewards. Although they attribute the losses to an unexplained fault, the underlying streak reset and its consequence are still a deliberate daily-reward rule under R11. No other game mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I've had 3 separate times times to where I've lost my 7 day streak for no reason whatsoever",
      "rule_applied": "T_DailyRewards definition and R11: a daily streak with a penalty for losing or missing it remains codable even when the reviewer attributes the loss to a bug or error.",
      "rationale": "The game maintains a 7-day streak whose loss penalizes the player's daily return, even though the reviewer says the resets occurred erroneously."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a daily mission system with a limited number of attempts and a real-time timer that takes nearly two minutes to replenish chances to play. The timer imposes an arbitrary wait before continued gameplay, matching Wait to Play under Playing by Appointment. The mention of daily missions does not by itself establish a daily login reward, streak, or punishment for missing a day, so Daily Rewards is not assigned. The complaint that items do not match orders is too unclear to establish a coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "The game makes the player wait for new play attempts to regenerate before continuing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a casino-style game where the player is expected to spend real money while pursuing wins. The phrase about coming back to spend more money despite it being impossible to win implies a monetized chance-based activity, which supports Gambling. The review does not describe a specific rigging mechanism, variable reward delivery, progression barrier, or in-game currency, so no other label applies.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "why would I ever come back and spend more money when it is impossible to when",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly connects spending real money with trying to win in a casino-slots game, indicating paid participation in a chance-based game for a possible reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes in-game advertisements that the player cannot exit once they appear, which compels the player to watch them. It also describes the ad becoming stuck and preventing further play after viewing; that part is attributed to a technical fault, so it does not independently trigger a dark-pattern label under R11. No payment option for removing the ads is mentioned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "cant back out of ads",
      "rule_applied": "definition; forced to watch ads",
      "rationale": "The player cannot dismiss the in-game advertisements, so viewing them is compelled."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
